This assert statement checks if the index of `x` before the operation is less than or equal to the index of `x` after the operation, or if `x` did not exist in the original list. This is based on the comment about the behavior of `LastIndexOf` method in C# documentation, under the section "LastIndexOf and IndexOf Return Values": "LastIndexOf method returns the zero-based index of the last occurrence of a value in an array, or -1 if value is not found in the array."

Please note that the assertion may fail if `index` being tested for is equal to `0` or beyond the list count when the condition for the statement to be true is reached. C#'s index-based collections such as ArrayList count from `0` and up. So, for example, in a list of length `3`, the last index would be `2`, the next would be `3`, and thus the 'last' index would be `3` - hence the assertion may fail in these edge cases. If you expect the assertions to fail at these indices, you should add additional conditions to prevent it from failing.
